# feature-nilpy-thirdparty-libraries-as-targets — relative imports INSIDE a
# pulled .py MODULE, which is a different code path from the one
# test_nilpy_relative_import.npy covers.
#
# That test puts the relative imports in the MAIN PROGRAM, which reaches them
# through PyParseImportRun's leading run and always worked. A real package
# writes them in its `__init__.py`, and a pulled module's statements go through
# PyParseStatement instead — whose import gate required an IDENTIFIER one token
# past `from`, so a leading dot fell out of import handling entirely and the
# statement parser met a bare `from` and called it an undefined variable.
#
# webencodings/__init__.py:19 (`from .labels import LABELS`) is what found it,
# and it blocked ALL FOUR fetched Python corpora, because a package that does
# not use relative imports internally is the exception.
#
# The two spellings are both exercised here: `from .mod import names` and the
# bare `from . import mod` with qualified access.
#
# `from .mod import NAME as ALIAS` is exercised too, and it is the reason U is
# here: inside a pulled module the alias SYMBOL was allocated and its binding
# never materialised, so the name read as 0 with no diagnostic. The only flush
# point was the main program's, and a module has its own body to emit into.
#
# RE-EXPORT is asserted too, and it was the harder half: a name the __init__.py
# obtained by IMPORTING it (A, B, RENAMED below) is read here through the
# package, not just names it defines (S, T, U). A from-import now BINDS in the
# importing module, which is what CPython does; before, flat unit scope stood in
# for that binding and stopped at the package boundary, so `from pkg import A`
# found nothing while the aliased spelling worked. That asymmetry is what
# identified the fix.
#
# One level only, on purpose: `from .subdir import X` where subdir is a
# DIRECTORY with its own __init__.py is still unresolved and has its own ticket.
from nilpy_relpkg import S, T, U, A, B, RENAMED

print("sum", S)
print("through-func", T)
print("through-alias", U)
print("re-exported", A, B, RENAMED)
